1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
From Japan to France, Germany to Spain, and now the United

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
States. Wheels on steel that can reach speeds

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
over 300 miles per hour. Unmatched in safety and efficiency,

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:22,000
they whisk millions of passengers between cities, countries,

5
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:27,000
and continents. Now, bullet trains on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It is America's own version of the bullet train, the Acela

7
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:48,180
Express.

8
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:51,160
Its name was created from the words acceleration and

9
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:54,620
excellence, and many believe the Acela, with its state of the art

10
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:58,320
design, power, and speed, could revolutionize the way we

11
00:00:58,321 --> 00:01:01,220
travel.

12
00:01:01,320 --> 00:01:08,260
The Acela was the truly first new train developed in America

13
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:11,860
in 30 years.

14
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:16,180
So we're basically breaking ground.

15
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:20,780
Launched in the winter of 2000 at a cost of $800 million,

16
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:24,780
the Acela rockets between Boston and Washington, DC,

17
00:01:24,781 --> 00:01:30,080
at speeds reaching 150 miles an hour.

18
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:32,780
The Acela takes about 2 1⁄2 hours to travel

19
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:37,660
between New York and Washington, and 3 hours and 18 minutes

20
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:40,760
from New York to Boston.

21
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:43,200
The New York-Boston leg is now about 90 minutes

22
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:46,360
faster than earlier trains.

23
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:47,640
It has power to spare.

24
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:52,000
It's kind of the sports car of the industry right now.

25
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:54,380
During the Acela's journey north of New York,

26
00:01:54,381 --> 00:01:57,380
twists and turns that if you were to fit them together,

27
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:00,520
they would create 10 full circles.

28
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:03,120
The Acela can take the turns at speeds higher

29
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:08,000
than conventional trains because of its unique tilting cars.

30
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:11,360
As the Acela enters a curve, a computer in the lead car

31
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:15,500
adjusts hydraulic jacks in the train's wheel sets.

32
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:19,180
As a result, the passenger cars will tilt by as much as four

33
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:22,600
degrees depending on the speed of the train and sharpness

34
00:02:22,601 --> 00:02:27,400
sharpness of the curve. We have a series of transponders

35
00:02:27,401 --> 00:02:32,400
that are embedded in the track structure that actually give

36
00:02:32,401 --> 00:02:36,400
location to the train so the train knows what it's supposed

37
00:02:36,401 --> 00:02:40,400
to be doing at various locations. Without this

38
00:02:40,401 --> 00:02:43,400
technology, passengers would have to brace themselves for

39
00:02:43,401 --> 00:02:46,400
every turn. The ideal high-speed rail

40
00:02:46,401 --> 00:02:50,400
experience is you're in a railroad car, but you're not

41
00:02:50,401 --> 00:02:56,400
subject to an acute sensation of speed.

42
00:02:56,500 --> 00:03:00,340
There's no drama associated with it.

43
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:02,340
The Acela belongs to an elite group

44
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:04,640
known as high-speed trains.

45
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:07,560
It's a term roughly defined as passenger trains that travel

46
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:11,060
above 200 kilometers, or 125 miles an hour.

47
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:18,500
And like most high-speed trains,

48
00:03:18,501 --> 00:03:21,400
the Acela is electrically powered.

49
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,040
The benefit is that electric trains tend to be lighter in

50
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:27,240
total weight than diesel locomotives, more economical

51
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:31,040
to operate, and they're able to achieve faster acceleration

52
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:36,860
and stopping. They acquire power from overhead

53
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:41,460
wires called the catenary. A current collector, or pantograph,

54
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:44,760
carries electricity to an engine that drives the train's traction

55
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:46,960
motors.

56
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:53,500
But as fast as the Acela is, it cannot travel at 150 miles an

57
00:03:53,501 --> 00:03:55,500
hour during its entire run.

58
00:03:55,501 --> 00:04:00,500
If it could, the Boston to Washington trip might be closer

59
00:04:00,501 --> 00:04:03,500
to four or even three hours.

60
00:04:03,501 --> 00:04:05,500
The principal culprit?

61
00:04:05,501 --> 00:04:07,500
It shares its route with other trains.

62
00:04:07,501 --> 00:04:10,500
We have numerous areas where we have constrained operation,

63
00:04:10,501 --> 00:04:11,500
New York in particular.

64
00:04:11,501 --> 00:04:15,500
Literally 1,000 trains go in and out of there during the day.

65
00:04:15,501 --> 00:04:16,740
during the day.

66
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:20,300
So anything that's moving a lot faster, a lot slower,

67
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:22,240
tends to be an operational problem.

68
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:28,440
Thousands of people travel on the Acela trains each day,

69
00:04:28,540 --> 00:04:29,640
and the number is growing.

70
00:04:32,260 --> 00:04:37,060
A one-way ticket from Boston to Washington is under $200.

71
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:40,800
Riders will find large windows that allow light to flood in,

72
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:44,360
oversized adjustable seats, ample legroom,

73
00:04:44,361 --> 00:04:47,660
and work tables with electrical outlets.

74
00:04:47,661 --> 00:04:49,860
Ridership is excellent for us.

75
00:04:49,861 --> 00:04:53,260
We anticipate growth over the years,

76
00:04:53,261 --> 00:04:57,700
and certainly all the features that we've incorporated

77
00:04:57,701 --> 00:05:01,320
in this particular train set will be used in train sets

78
00:05:01,321 --> 00:05:03,620
that will follow us.

79
00:05:03,621 --> 00:05:09,360
High-speed trains are among today's fastest land vehicles.

80
00:05:09,361 --> 00:05:11,660
And yet it was not so long ago in human history

81
00:05:11,661 --> 00:05:15,500
could only go as fast as our feet or animals could carry us.

82
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:23,500
The ancient Greeks and later the Romans used what might be called

83
00:05:23,501 --> 00:05:27,500
the first railroads. They built stone grooves for

84
00:05:27,501 --> 00:05:32,620
wagon wheels. By the late medieval period,

85
00:05:32,621 --> 00:05:35,820
German miners were using wooden tracks with rails and special

86
00:05:35,821 --> 00:05:40,620
wooden wheels to haul lead and tin from mines.

87
00:05:40,621 --> 00:05:44,860
whether it's wood or metal or anything else, traveling in the

88
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:50,360
dirt requires a lot of effort to move.

89
00:05:50,460 --> 00:05:53,360
The idea of building tracks spread to other parts of

90
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:57,380
Europe, and eventually rails and wheels were made of cast iron.

91
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:01,300
Then, in the late 1700s, came the invention that would one

92
00:06:01,400 --> 00:06:07,280
day revolutionize rail travel, the steam engine.

93
00:06:07,281 --> 00:06:12,980
By the period of 185 to 1810, it improved enough that boilers

94
00:06:12,981 --> 00:06:18,920
could get smaller, and the idea of self-propelled engines,

95
00:06:18,921 --> 00:06:24,340
locomotives, became a conceptual possibility.

96
00:06:24,341 --> 00:06:27,960
In 1829, a self-taught mining engineer named George

97
00:06:27,961 --> 00:06:32,380
Stevenson turned a conceptual possibility into a reality by

98
00:06:32,381 --> 00:06:35,640
creating what would become the standard for steam locomotives

99
00:06:35,641 --> 00:06:40,940
locomotives around the world. It was called the Rocket.

100
00:06:41,040 --> 00:06:44,140
The Rocket had a smokestack in the front and a boiler with a

101
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:47,880
firebox in the back. The steam it generated would cause

102
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:51,380
pistons to move back and forth in cylinders, which were

103
00:06:51,480 --> 00:06:57,200
connected to crankpins on the driving wheels.

104
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:00,500
The Rocket could pull dozens of railroad cars weighing hundreds

105
00:07:00,501 --> 00:07:06,180
tons at the unheard of speed of 20 to 25 miles an hour.

106
00:07:06,181 --> 00:07:09,740
It was the first reliable, long distance, all weather form

107
00:07:09,741 --> 00:07:12,380
of mechanical land transportation.

108
00:07:12,381 --> 00:07:16,020
And it had a transforming effect on England

109
00:07:16,021 --> 00:07:17,860
and the rest of the world.

110
00:07:17,861 --> 00:07:19,580
But the new trains after the rocket

111
00:07:19,581 --> 00:07:23,060
were far from being the most reliable form of transportation,

112
00:07:23,061 --> 00:07:26,300
or the safest, in Europe or the United States.

113
00:07:26,301 --> 00:07:29,820
By the 1840s, locomotive pulled trains

114
00:07:29,821 --> 00:07:33,160
were routinely traveling at 60 and 70 miles an hour

115
00:07:33,260 --> 00:07:35,500
on some railroad lines in the United States.

116
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:38,500
This was in an era of primitive braking,

117
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:42,700
fragile wooden cars, poorly constructed,

118
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:45,440
poorly maintained track.

119
00:07:45,540 --> 00:07:47,380
The consequences of any accident

120
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:48,800
were generally catastrophic.

121
00:07:52,080 --> 00:07:55,980
Bridges would burn down that no one would know,

122
00:07:56,080 --> 00:07:58,380
and trains would hurtle off them in the dark.

123
00:07:58,381 --> 00:08:01,620
would collapse under the weight of trains.

124
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:04,780
The primitive rail might become detached

125
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:07,380
and spring up to the floor of a car

126
00:08:07,480 --> 00:08:12,320
and skewer whatever poor occupant happened to be in the way.

127
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:13,920
For workers on the trains,

128
00:08:14,020 --> 00:08:16,940
even the act of slowing down was dangerous.

129
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,340
All braking was done by hand.

130
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:23,140
The job of the brakeman was to physically apply the brakes

131
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:26,840
by generally turning a wheel that tightened the chain that

132
00:08:26,841 --> 00:08:30,580
that pulled brake shoes against the wheels of the cars.

133
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:32,940
Well, clearly, you couldn't stop the train quickly

134
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:34,740
in an emergency if that was the case.

135
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:40,080
Then in 1869, George Westinghouse

136
00:08:40,180 --> 00:08:43,400
introduced the automatic brake system.

137
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:45,300
His invention used compressed air

138
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:47,200
to apply pressure to brake valves.

139
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:53,300
In the same year, the United States

140
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:56,840
finished building its first transcontinental railroad.

141
00:08:56,841 --> 00:09:04,380
on May 10, 1869, and the event ignited a railroad boom.

142
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:07,560
In the short period between 1860 and 1900,

143
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:09,720
the network of tracks in the United States

144
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:14,620
grew from 30,000 miles to 200,000 miles.

145
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:17,660
The steam railroad, which allowed people for the first

146
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:20,740
time to travel unimaginable distances at remarkably high

147
00:09:20,840 --> 00:09:25,640
rates of speed, made it conceptually possible to imagine

148
00:09:25,641 --> 00:09:30,640
thousands of miles of filling this vast continent.

149
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:38,620
Railroad technology continued to improve,

150
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:41,660
and by the First World War, all the pieces that make up the

151
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:45,620
modern railroad were in place.

152
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:47,700
Automatic air brakes, steel rails,

153
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:52,260
telegraphic communications, automatic track signals,

154
00:09:52,261 --> 00:09:56,560
and the steel passenger car.

155
00:09:56,561 --> 00:10:01,300
By the 1920s, it was commonplace for high-speed,

156
00:10:01,301 --> 00:10:05,300
deluxe passenger trains in this country to routinely travel 70

157
00:10:05,301 --> 00:10:08,500
and 80 miles an hour over short distances.

158
00:10:08,501 --> 00:10:13,420
By the 1930s, some railroads were running trains routinely

159
00:10:13,421 --> 00:10:19,420
at speeds of over 100 miles an hour, safely and efficiently.

160
00:10:19,421 --> 00:10:23,420
steam engines dominated the rails.

161
00:10:23,421 --> 00:10:27,420
But in the mid-1930s, a new type of locomotive could be seen

162
00:10:27,421 --> 00:10:34,420
streaking across the nation, the diesel electric engine.

163
00:10:34,421 --> 00:10:37,420
While not necessarily much faster than steam engines,

164
00:10:37,421 --> 00:10:41,420
diesel electrics were more economical.

165
00:10:41,421 --> 00:10:44,420
Steam locomotives were very labor intensive.

166
00:10:44,421 --> 00:10:48,420
It took perhaps two or three hours of service and maintenance

167
00:10:48,421 --> 00:10:52,420
maintenance for every operating hour, whereas diesel engines,

168
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:55,420
in many respects, were as reliable and simple

169
00:10:55,520 --> 00:10:56,720
as an automobile.

170
00:10:56,820 --> 00:11:01,360
You turn the key, you fire them up, and you go.

171
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:03,960
From the beginning, they became industry favorites

172
00:11:04,060 --> 00:11:06,780
and were quickly put to work pulling some of America's most

173
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:09,980
glamorous trains.

174
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:13,180
In spite of this efficiency and flourishing of technology,

175
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:15,420
passenger rail service in the United States

176
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:18,420
began to decline.

177
00:11:18,421 --> 00:11:23,060
In 1956, the decline gained momentum after President Dwight

178
00:11:23,160 --> 00:11:26,020
D. Eisenhower signed the National System of Interstate

179
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:28,960
and Defense Highways Act.

180
00:11:29,060 --> 00:11:33,660
The bill would create a network of superhighways 40,000 miles

181
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:35,180
long.

182
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:38,680
Railroads were left to fend for themselves.

183
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:43,220
And as more Americans turned to their cars into air travel,

184
00:11:43,320 --> 00:11:46,220
rail companies shifted their focus to the more lucrative end

185
00:11:46,221 --> 00:11:48,820
to the more lucrative end of their business,

186
00:11:48,821 --> 00:11:53,520
freight service, leaving passenger service to languish.

187
00:11:53,521 --> 00:11:57,520
And finally, in 1971, the government

188
00:11:57,521 --> 00:11:59,180
struck a deal with the freight railroads

189
00:11:59,181 --> 00:12:01,980
to assume all responsibility for intercity passenger

190
00:12:01,981 --> 00:12:03,380
service.

191
00:12:03,381 --> 00:12:05,260
And that was the start of the current system,

192
00:12:05,261 --> 00:12:08,620
where you have Amtrak as the single national provider

193
00:12:08,621 --> 00:12:09,720
of intercity rail service.

194
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:15,280
On the day Amtrak started operation,

195
00:12:15,281 --> 00:12:18,880
the number of daily passenger trains to fewer than 200,

196
00:12:18,980 --> 00:12:23,820
effectively reducing service from 43,000 miles to 16,000.

197
00:12:27,720 --> 00:12:30,520
Other industrialized nations struggled with what to do with

198
00:12:30,620 --> 00:12:35,440
their passenger rail service. And if it weren't for a

199
00:12:35,540 --> 00:12:40,680
strong-willed 70-year-old man in Japan named Shinji Sogo, the

200
00:12:40,780 --> 00:12:45,280
world's first bullet train might never have been born.

201
00:12:45,281 --> 00:12:49,280
The fastest steam locomotive was the UK's Mallard.

202
00:12:49,281 --> 00:12:55,280
It reached a speed of 126 miles per hour on July 3, 1938.

203
00:12:55,281 --> 00:13:02,280
Bullet trains will return on Modern Marvels.

204
00:13:02,281 --> 00:13:09,280
We now return to Bullet Trains on Modern Marvels.

205
00:13:09,281 --> 00:13:12,280
Few trains have captured the imagination of the world, like

206
00:13:12,281 --> 00:13:16,620
like Japan's bullet train. These streamlined and futuristic

207
00:13:16,720 --> 00:13:19,880
looking passenger trains race from one end of Japan to the

208
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:26,920
other at speeds ranging from 130 to 186 miles an hour.

209
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:30,740
They carry more than 130 million passengers each year.

210
00:13:35,740 --> 00:13:38,440
Ironically, the country with one of the most advanced train

211
00:13:38,441 --> 00:13:42,080
systems in the world today was one of the last industrialized

212
00:13:42,081 --> 00:13:51,080
nations to have a railroad. Up until the mid-19th century,

213
00:13:51,081 --> 00:13:55,200
no one in Japan had ever seen a train.

214
00:13:55,201 --> 00:13:57,800
It was only when America's Commodore Perry returned to

215
00:13:57,801 --> 00:14:02,200
Japan in 1854 for a second visit that the Japanese got

216
00:14:02,201 --> 00:14:06,800
their first glimpse. Among the gifts that he

217
00:14:06,801 --> 00:14:12,240
brought was a toy train, and the Japanese had never seen

218
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:18,040
anything like it, and they were fascinated by the train.

219
00:14:18,140 --> 00:14:20,740
Japan fully embraced Victorian locomotives

220
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:24,060
and rail technology.

221
00:14:24,160 --> 00:14:29,320
From 1864 to 1900, railroads were built throughout Japan

222
00:14:29,420 --> 00:14:32,900
and soon linked most major cities.

223
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:36,800
By the early 20th century, the government agency overcame

224
00:14:36,801 --> 00:14:39,700
was the country's largest employer.

225
00:14:42,700 --> 00:14:48,740
In 1909, that agency hired graduate student Shinji Sogo.

226
00:14:48,741 --> 00:14:52,040
He started out as a minor office clerk, but he would later be

227
00:14:52,041 --> 00:14:54,560
known as the father of the bullet train.

228
00:14:57,560 --> 00:15:01,500
Sogo was born in 1884 in a small village 31 years after

229
00:15:01,501 --> 00:15:05,000
Commodore Perry first landed in Japan.

230
00:15:05,001 --> 00:15:09,000
As a student, he attended some of Japan's best schools.

231
00:15:09,001 --> 00:15:15,000
He was a man of a lot of ideas, ingenious, mischievous.

232
00:15:15,001 --> 00:15:20,000
When he got to Tokyo to college, he realized the opportunity ahead of him,

233
00:15:20,001 --> 00:15:24,000
and he really applied himself.

234
00:15:24,001 --> 00:15:29,000
After landing the job with the railroads, Sogo climbed up through the ranks.

235
00:15:29,001 --> 00:15:34,000
By 1930, he was appointed director of the South Manchuria Railway in China.

236
00:15:34,001 --> 00:15:39,000
Japan controlled Manchuria after winning the Russo-Japanese War

237
00:15:39,001 --> 00:15:41,000
of 1905.

238
00:15:41,001 --> 00:15:44,000
They depended on the railroads there for getting food and raw

239
00:15:44,001 --> 00:15:45,000
materials back to Japan.

240
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:52,000
But in 1934, Manchuria's political environment became

241
00:15:52,001 --> 00:15:55,000
increasingly unstable, and Sogo resigned.

242
00:15:55,001 --> 00:15:59,000
He returned to Japan to work as a civil servant until his

243
00:15:59,001 --> 00:16:01,000
retirement in 1948.

244
00:16:01,001 --> 00:16:05,000
His story might have ended there if it weren't for a crisis in

245
00:16:05,001 --> 00:16:09,000
Japan's railroads at the end of World War II.

246
00:16:09,001 --> 00:16:14,000
The Japanese railroads at the time of the surrender in 1945

247
00:16:14,001 --> 00:16:19,000
were in terrible shape. The country had been at war for

248
00:16:19,001 --> 00:16:22,000
almost 12 years. The railroads had been

249
00:16:22,001 --> 00:16:28,000
overloaded. They had not been maintained.

250
00:16:28,001 --> 00:16:33,000
Tracks, bridges, and safety equipment had deteriorated, and

251
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:35,800
there were few resources available to repair them.

252
00:16:35,900 --> 00:16:39,940
Worse still, there was no highway system to speak of, and Japan

253
00:16:40,040 --> 00:16:42,840
relied heavily on trains for transportation.

254
00:16:42,940 --> 00:16:45,360
They were dangerously overcrowded.

255
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:53,600
Railroad conditions continued to worsen until 1955, when Shinji

256
00:16:53,700 --> 00:16:58,000
Sogo was asked to take over as head of Japan National Railroad.

257
00:16:58,001 --> 00:17:00,000
The decision surprised the nation.

258
00:17:00,001 --> 00:17:02,500
He was 70 years old.

259
00:17:02,501 --> 00:17:05,000
But Sogo had a vision for Japan's railroads

260
00:17:05,001 --> 00:17:09,000
and was determined to see it through.

261
00:17:09,001 --> 00:17:15,000
His chief idea was to build what was called a Shinkansen,

262
00:17:15,001 --> 00:17:21,000
which literally means the New Rail Line.

263
00:17:21,001 --> 00:17:27,000
He felt that there was a need for an efficient, high-speed rail

264
00:17:27,001 --> 00:17:32,240
high-speed rail line between the two large cities of Osaka and

265
00:17:32,241 --> 00:17:35,400
Tokyo. The new line would cover a

266
00:17:35,401 --> 00:17:40,080
distance of 320 miles and be completely dedicated to passenger

267
00:17:40,081 --> 00:17:43,720
service. The cost of Sogo's plan would

268
00:17:43,721 --> 00:17:49,560
still be astronomical. Land in Japan is very,

269
00:17:49,561 --> 00:17:52,760
very scarce, very, very expensive.

270
00:17:52,761 --> 00:17:56,920
They also had to develop a new technology, build new

271
00:17:56,921 --> 00:18:00,920
build an electrical system that would provide the power for this

272
00:18:00,921 --> 00:18:04,920
kind of a line. And so he knew that he would have

273
00:18:04,921 --> 00:18:10,920
a very difficult time getting an appropriation from the government.

274
00:18:10,921 --> 00:18:14,920
To push the project forward, Sogo did two things.

275
00:18:14,921 --> 00:18:19,920
He gave a false, low-cost estimate to win government support.

276
00:18:19,921 --> 00:18:23,920
And then he did what most entrepreneurs do, got a loan.

277
00:18:23,921 --> 00:18:28,120
Sloan, Sogo convinced the World Bank that Japan was still a

278
00:18:28,121 --> 00:18:31,520
developing nation, and he received $80 million.

279
00:18:31,521 --> 00:18:38,160
In the meantime, Sogo's engineers went to work.

280
00:18:38,161 --> 00:18:42,080
What they did is they took proven technology being used in

281
00:18:42,081 --> 00:18:46,880
Japan and essentially spruced it up and put a new image on it.

282
00:18:46,881 --> 00:18:51,680
The trains are what we call electrical multiple unit trains.

283
00:18:51,681 --> 00:18:54,520
different than what you'd find in any electrified commuter

284
00:18:54,521 --> 00:18:58,280
railway. They put bigger traction motors

285
00:18:58,281 --> 00:19:00,920
and better running gear under there for the higher speeds,

286
00:19:00,921 --> 00:19:06,320
different gearing. On most trains, heavy

287
00:19:06,321 --> 00:19:08,920
traction motors are found in the lead car.

288
00:19:08,921 --> 00:19:12,440
What engineers in Japan did was distribute the traction motors

289
00:19:12,441 --> 00:19:16,440
throughout the entire train, cutting down the weight in front.

290
00:19:16,441 --> 00:19:21,360
The weight distribution tactic of putting more

291
00:19:21,361 --> 00:19:25,000
motors in every car also gave the train a high horsepower

292
00:19:25,100 --> 00:19:30,260
to weight ratio. This made the Shinkansen very

293
00:19:30,360 --> 00:19:32,900
fast. It would remain a key design

294
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:36,800
feature in all Japanese high-speed trains to follow.

295
00:19:36,900 --> 00:19:39,520
This in every step of development was methodical

296
00:19:39,620 --> 00:19:42,920
and pragmatic. This was not a research

297
00:19:43,020 --> 00:19:45,820
program. This was a viable transportation

298
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:50,620
project to solve circulation and transportation needs.

299
00:19:50,621 --> 00:19:54,120
They built it fast, and they didn't want to take any risk.

300
00:19:54,220 --> 00:19:55,920
But they were smart, and they went out

301
00:19:56,020 --> 00:19:57,460
and hired a good industrial designer

302
00:19:57,560 --> 00:19:59,460
to put a nose on this train.

303
00:19:59,560 --> 00:20:03,740
And hence, the term bullet train became the legacy

304
00:20:03,840 --> 00:20:05,240
of the railway business.

305
00:20:08,500 --> 00:20:14,040
The bullet train was officially launched on October 1964.

306
00:20:14,140 --> 00:20:16,880
It was an instant success.

307
00:20:16,980 --> 00:20:19,180
Everybody wanted bullet trains.

308
00:20:19,181 --> 00:20:21,640
And that's truly probably the only train that looked like a

309
00:20:21,740 --> 00:20:24,180
bullet, but everything that's come after that is still

310
00:20:24,280 --> 00:20:26,220
called the bullet train.

311
00:20:26,320 --> 00:20:30,360
And they basically pushed their envelope from, let's say,

312
00:20:30,460 --> 00:20:33,160
90 miles an hour to 120.

313
00:20:33,260 --> 00:20:38,400
It was very high speed for 1964.

314
00:20:38,500 --> 00:20:41,000
It created a fantasy that all these other countries,

315
00:20:41,100 --> 00:20:43,400
the United States included, said, we have to do that too.

316
00:20:43,401 --> 00:20:50,340
In 1981, Sogo died of pneumonia at age 97.

317
00:20:50,440 --> 00:20:54,040
But before his death, he received Japan's highest

318
00:20:54,140 --> 00:20:55,240
civilian award.

319
00:20:59,340 --> 00:21:03,160
Since the first Tokyo-Osaka line in 1964,

320
00:21:03,260 --> 00:21:06,660
Japan has continually added to its high speed network

321
00:21:06,760 --> 00:21:10,460
and replaced its old trains with new.

322
00:21:10,461 --> 00:21:14,160
Today, there are more than 1,000 miles of high-speed lines

323
00:21:14,260 --> 00:21:20,100
and 360 bullet trains that serve 350,000 passengers

324
00:21:20,200 --> 00:21:22,600
each day.

325
00:21:22,700 --> 00:21:24,700
They're now up over 200 miles an hour

326
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:26,600
in some of the sections.

327
00:21:26,700 --> 00:21:30,520
They've gone to more higher horsepower train sets.

328
00:21:30,620 --> 00:21:32,940
The newest trains are very space age

329
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:36,520
in that they've been designed for the minimum aerodynamic

330
00:21:36,620 --> 00:21:37,860
drag.

331
00:21:37,861 --> 00:21:39,560
Some people say that this can't be real,

332
00:21:39,660 --> 00:21:41,060
but they're out there and running.

333
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:47,600
They have carried more than 2.8 billion passengers

334
00:21:47,700 --> 00:21:50,200
without a single fatality.

335
00:21:50,300 --> 00:21:53,540
There are no grade crossings or extreme curves.

336
00:21:53,640 --> 00:21:57,140
Both measures reduce the chance of collision or derailment.

337
00:21:57,240 --> 00:22:00,020
Furthermore, unlike America's high-speed Acela,

338
00:22:00,120 --> 00:22:02,560
it does not share its tracks with slower freight

339
00:22:02,660 --> 00:22:03,560
or passenger trains.

340
00:22:07,860 --> 00:22:11,860
There's also an army of several hundred workers inspecting the

341
00:22:11,861 --> 00:22:15,860
length of the track each night. Overhead wires are checked and

342
00:22:15,861 --> 00:22:18,860
track is replaced. And every 10 days, a train called

343
00:22:18,861 --> 00:22:23,860
Dr. Yellow runs diagnostic tests on both rail and wire using

344
00:22:23,861 --> 00:22:29,860
electronic monitoring equipment. One of the newest trains in

345
00:22:29,861 --> 00:22:35,860
service is the Nozami series 700 train.

346
00:22:35,861 --> 00:22:40,260
Nozami's operating speed is about 177 miles per hour,

347
00:22:40,360 --> 00:22:43,260
but it's capable of reaching 200.

348
00:22:43,360 --> 00:22:46,400
It's aerodynamically designed with a flattened nose that

349
00:22:46,500 --> 00:22:51,300
actually prevents the train from lifting up.

350
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:56,420
At 708 tons and made with a lightweight aluminum alloy,

351
00:22:56,520 --> 00:22:59,920
the Nozami is Japan's lightest train yet.

352
00:23:00,020 --> 00:23:02,320
But as light and swift as the Nozami is,

353
00:23:02,420 --> 00:23:05,860
there's something faster.

354
00:23:05,861 --> 00:23:12,860
It's called the TGV, or Train à Grande Vitesse.

355
00:23:12,861 --> 00:23:17,860
It's French for high-speed train, and in any language, it moves.

356
00:23:17,861 --> 00:23:21,860
In the first 30 years of operation,

357
00:23:21,861 --> 00:23:26,860
bullet trains covered more than 650 million miles, or 1,300

358
00:23:26,861 --> 00:23:30,860
round trips between the Earth and the Moon.

359
00:23:30,861 --> 00:23:34,860
Bullet trains will return on Modern Marvels.

360
00:23:35,860 --> 00:23:40,020
We now return to bullet trains

361
00:23:40,021 --> 00:23:41,100
on Modern Marvels.

362
00:23:44,100 --> 00:23:47,100
The fastest conventional train in the world today

363
00:23:47,101 --> 00:23:50,860
is a TGV, or Train à grande vitesse.

364
00:23:50,861 --> 00:23:54,620
In 1990, the TGV Atlantique broke all records

365
00:23:54,621 --> 00:23:57,300
by reaching 320 miles an hour.

366
00:24:01,780 --> 00:24:04,100
The speed of a regularly scheduled TGV

367
00:24:04,101 --> 00:24:07,500
is considerably slower, but the train still turns heads

368
00:24:07,600 --> 00:24:09,340
when flying through the countryside

369
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:12,540
at a cruising speed of 186 miles an hour.

370
00:24:19,240 --> 00:24:24,260
The TGV was introduced in 1981 by the French National Railways,

371
00:24:24,360 --> 00:24:28,100
or SNCF. The trains are here today

372
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:31,800
because of steps taken in the 1960s by the agency

373
00:24:31,801 --> 00:24:35,400
to head off a crisis in the French railroad industry.

374
00:24:35,500 --> 00:24:39,000
The French railroads were condemned.

375
00:24:39,100 --> 00:24:42,240
I mean, if you don't improve yourself at a time where you

376
00:24:42,340 --> 00:24:46,040
have a big expansion of road and air travel,

377
00:24:46,140 --> 00:24:48,360
you just disappear.

378
00:24:48,460 --> 00:24:51,160
Unlike passenger trains in the United States,

379
00:24:51,260 --> 00:24:53,760
French trains were still one of the most popular forms of

380
00:24:53,860 --> 00:24:57,800
transportation in France. But ridership on lines between

381
00:24:57,801 --> 00:25:01,800
the largest cities was at the saturation point.

382
00:25:01,801 --> 00:25:04,840
In addition, train cars were decades old.

383
00:25:04,841 --> 00:25:09,320
So for the SNCF management, it was quite a challenge,

384
00:25:09,321 --> 00:25:11,880
not only to manage a day-to-day business,

385
00:25:11,881 --> 00:25:15,080
but also to come up with a vision for the future that

386
00:25:15,081 --> 00:25:19,880
would be compelling and exciting enough.

387
00:25:19,881 --> 00:25:25,960
Remember, in the 60s, Concorde was being built.

388
00:25:25,961 --> 00:25:29,500
There was a great expansion of the highways in France.

389
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:32,360
And you want the railroads not to do anything

390
00:25:32,460 --> 00:25:33,900
to react about that?

391
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,400
They have to invent something.

392
00:25:36,500 --> 00:25:39,000
One idea was the aero train.

393
00:25:39,100 --> 00:25:42,200
It was a jet-powered train that ran on a cushion of air,

394
00:25:42,300 --> 00:25:43,320
much like a hovercraft.

395
00:25:47,080 --> 00:25:50,560
In experiments, it had reached speeds over 100 miles an hour

396
00:25:50,660 --> 00:25:55,220
and had a great deal of support because it was futuristic.

397
00:25:55,221 --> 00:25:59,520
But it was the less exotic turbo trains that won out.

398
00:25:59,521 --> 00:26:02,300
French engineers were certain they could create a train with

399
00:26:02,301 --> 00:26:05,000
even greater speed and performance.

400
00:26:05,001 --> 00:26:12,240
And in early 1970, they developed the TGV-001.

401
00:26:12,241 --> 00:26:17,940
In 1972, powered by a gas turbine, the TGV-001 set a world

402
00:26:17,941 --> 00:26:23,140
record with the incredible speed of 198 miles an hour.

403
00:26:23,141 --> 00:26:26,380
The TTV was an absolute revolution.

404
00:26:26,480 --> 00:26:31,880
It was the end result of the ability to master technology

405
00:26:31,980 --> 00:26:36,520
to a point that was not conceivable in most minds

406
00:26:36,620 --> 00:26:39,020
before.

407
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:42,200
Engineers had created a train that was light in weight

408
00:26:42,300 --> 00:26:44,060
and harnessed it to a powerful engine.

409
00:26:47,860 --> 00:26:51,760
To cut down on tonnage, they reduced the number of axles.

410
00:26:51,761 --> 00:26:56,000
Most conventional trains have a pair of two axle trucks per car.

411
00:26:56,001 --> 00:26:58,500
On a TGV, the axles are shared.

412
00:26:58,501 --> 00:27:03,800
The ends of two cars rest on a common axle truck.

413
00:27:03,801 --> 00:27:07,100
Aerodynamics are improved due to the lower weight of the train

414
00:27:07,101 --> 00:27:11,920
and smaller gaps between cars.

415
00:27:11,921 --> 00:27:17,520
This design is present in all TGVs.

416
00:27:17,521 --> 00:27:20,720
But the global oil crisis of 1973

417
00:27:20,721 --> 00:27:24,960
suddenly made turbo TGVs very costly.

418
00:27:25,060 --> 00:27:28,660
During the same year, the TGV became electric.

419
00:27:28,760 --> 00:27:30,720
And like the bullet train in Japan,

420
00:27:30,820 --> 00:27:35,660
the TGV would obtain its power from overhead wires.

421
00:27:35,760 --> 00:27:38,580
In order to exploit the technology possibilities

422
00:27:38,680 --> 00:27:42,280
of the TGV concept, there was a need to build new tracks.

423
00:27:48,180 --> 00:27:50,720
The SNCF chose to build a dedicated

424
00:27:50,721 --> 00:27:55,720
speed route 256 miles long between Paris and France's

425
00:27:55,820 --> 00:27:59,220
second largest city, Lyon.

426
00:27:59,320 --> 00:28:02,260
Construction began in 1976.

427
00:28:02,360 --> 00:28:05,040
Costs would be kept down because the new line would use

428
00:28:05,140 --> 00:28:08,800
existing tracks for the first 18 miles outside of Paris

429
00:28:08,900 --> 00:28:11,440
and share the infrastructure of two roadways,

430
00:28:11,540 --> 00:28:14,380
eliminating the need for extensive excavation.

431
00:28:14,480 --> 00:28:16,540
The rest of the route would be a straight shot,

432
00:28:16,640 --> 00:28:20,720
crossing vast open stretches of countryside.

433
00:28:20,721 --> 00:28:27,720
The first section of high-speed rail between Paris and Lyon

434
00:28:27,721 --> 00:28:33,720
opened September 27, 1981, and TGVs painted bright orange

435
00:28:33,721 --> 00:28:38,720
were soon running 164 miles an hour along its rails.

436
00:28:38,721 --> 00:28:41,720
They won rave reviews.

437
00:28:41,721 --> 00:28:49,720
I think that train had in itself such an emotional

438
00:28:49,721 --> 00:28:52,120
and emotional content.

439
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:56,500
It has definitely led the way for many other projects,

440
00:28:56,600 --> 00:28:58,400
not only in France, but also in Europe.

441
00:29:01,460 --> 00:29:04,200
Today, France has the world's most extensive network

442
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:07,140
of high-speed rail in the world.

443
00:29:07,240 --> 00:29:09,740
It's a country about the size of Texas,

444
00:29:09,840 --> 00:29:12,980
but has nearly 1,000 miles of high-speed track,

445
00:29:13,080 --> 00:29:15,420
and its network is growing.

446
00:29:15,520 --> 00:29:18,240
Success has been astounding.

447
00:29:18,241 --> 00:29:21,000
And with the link from Paris to the channel tunnel

448
00:29:21,100 --> 00:29:23,980
toward England, they now have high-speed rail

449
00:29:24,080 --> 00:29:26,240
from coast at the English Channel

450
00:29:26,340 --> 00:29:29,580
to coast on the Mediterranean.

451
00:29:29,680 --> 00:29:32,380
TGVs have also proven to be safe.

452
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:34,420
After more than 20 years of operation

453
00:29:34,520 --> 00:29:37,100
and over 17 million riders a year,

454
00:29:37,200 --> 00:29:38,900
there have been no fatalities.

455
00:29:42,700 --> 00:29:48,240
So successful, they've spawned an entire industry in France.

456
00:29:48,241 --> 00:29:53,240
From a workshop outside of Paris that provides daily maintenance

457
00:29:53,340 --> 00:29:56,840
to the Alstom factory in the coastal town of La Rochelle

458
00:29:56,940 --> 00:29:59,780
that manufactures double-decker TGVs.

459
00:29:59,880 --> 00:30:02,080
You cannot fault the French for not

460
00:30:02,180 --> 00:30:06,760
doing a fine job, a quality job, in putting out a product that's

461
00:30:06,860 --> 00:30:11,600
been basically one of the models of the world.

462
00:30:11,700 --> 00:30:14,200
And if you ever want to sell a train system here,

463
00:30:14,300 --> 00:30:18,240
you point to France and say, we ought to have that too.

464
00:30:18,241 --> 00:30:21,540
And while today's high-speed trains like the TGV provide

465
00:30:21,640 --> 00:30:24,640
the fastest rail service in the world,

466
00:30:24,740 --> 00:30:26,740
there are many who believe there's a quicker way

467
00:30:26,840 --> 00:30:31,580
for trains to travel, one that doesn't involve steel wheels

468
00:30:31,680 --> 00:30:32,380
or rail.

469
00:30:35,700 --> 00:30:38,600
In trips 350 miles or less, the TGV

470
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:42,100
captures 80% to 90% of the French travel market,

471
00:30:42,200 --> 00:30:45,800
surpassing commercial airline service.

472
00:30:45,801 --> 00:30:48,800
Bullet Trains will return on Modern Marvels.

473
00:30:53,300 --> 00:30:55,800
We now return to Bullet Trains on Modern Marvels.

474
00:31:03,800 --> 00:31:07,800
On April 14, 1999, an experimental train in Japan

475
00:31:07,801 --> 00:31:12,800
called the MLX-01 smashed a world speed record.

476
00:31:12,801 --> 00:31:16,800
Traveling at an astonishing 345 miles per hour,

477
00:31:16,900 --> 00:31:21,300
no wheels guided its movement, and no engine powered its cars.

478
00:31:21,400 --> 00:31:24,040
It moved entirely by magnetic force.

479
00:31:24,140 --> 00:31:27,640
The train is known as a maglev.

480
00:31:27,740 --> 00:31:30,320
There are no maglevs in commercial service in the world

481
00:31:30,420 --> 00:31:34,180
today, but Japan, China, and even companies in Germany

482
00:31:34,280 --> 00:31:38,360
and the United States hope to change that.

483
00:31:38,460 --> 00:31:41,600
The idea behind maglevs is simple.

484
00:31:41,601 --> 00:31:43,900
Put two attracting magnets near each other,

485
00:31:43,901 --> 00:31:46,900
and they clamp together. Try the same thing with two

486
00:31:46,901 --> 00:31:49,700
repelling magnets, and they push apart.

487
00:31:49,701 --> 00:31:53,300
This principle is used to levitate trains and guide trains

488
00:31:53,301 --> 00:32:00,200
above a special track. Maglevs run on a special guideway

489
00:32:00,201 --> 00:32:04,400
lined with magnets that change polarity from north to south.

490
00:32:04,401 --> 00:32:07,500
The maglev is propelled forward as magnets in the guideway

491
00:32:07,501 --> 00:32:10,300
attract and repel magnets located on the train.

492
00:32:12,600 --> 00:32:16,300
It's an incredibly quiet ride, because you don't really

493
00:32:16,301 --> 00:32:17,400
hear them go by.

494
00:32:17,401 --> 00:32:22,240
You hear the rush of the wind, but since there's no moving

495
00:32:22,241 --> 00:32:25,760
motor, it's all magnetic, all you hear is the aerodynamic

496
00:32:25,761 --> 00:32:26,660
noise.

497
00:32:26,661 --> 00:32:27,860
There is no friction.

498
00:32:27,861 --> 00:32:31,460
There is no contact on the infrastructure.

499
00:32:31,461 --> 00:32:37,500
So there are a lot of benefits at first around the concept.

500
00:32:37,501 --> 00:32:42,500
The only issue I personally see with mag life

501
00:32:42,501 --> 00:32:45,740
is it is absolutely not compatible with anything

502
00:32:45,741 --> 00:32:48,440
that exists today.

503
00:32:48,441 --> 00:32:50,240
The idea that it is so different

504
00:32:50,241 --> 00:32:52,140
has attracted supporters.

505
00:32:52,141 --> 00:32:54,960
It may be the first new transportation technology

506
00:32:54,961 --> 00:32:55,960
in 100 years.

507
00:32:58,660 --> 00:33:00,360
There were some inventions in this country

508
00:33:00,361 --> 00:33:04,760
and ideas that magnetic forces can be used for accelerating

509
00:33:04,761 --> 00:33:06,760
and powering the vehicles.

510
00:33:06,761 --> 00:33:10,960
But it was really Germany in the 1970s that began to apply that

511
00:33:11,060 --> 00:33:14,660
and to build magnetic levitation vehicles.

512
00:33:14,760 --> 00:33:17,300
Today, a German company called TransRapid

513
00:33:17,400 --> 00:33:20,000
conducts maglev studies on this 20-mile track

514
00:33:20,100 --> 00:33:23,100
in Emsland, West Germany.

515
00:33:23,200 --> 00:33:25,520
The levitation magnets on the TransRapid system

516
00:33:25,620 --> 00:33:27,920
are located on the train's wraparound arms

517
00:33:28,020 --> 00:33:29,520
and on the guideway.

518
00:33:29,620 --> 00:33:31,460
These magnets propel the train.

519
00:33:31,560 --> 00:33:34,760
They also keep the train suspended with magnets on the side.

520
00:33:34,761 --> 00:33:37,860
Together, they keep the train at a uniform distance of 10

521
00:33:37,960 --> 00:33:39,260
millimeters from the guideway.

522
00:33:43,860 --> 00:33:48,340
Transrapid engineers have run the train to speeds of up to 271

523
00:33:48,440 --> 00:33:51,080
miles per hour.

524
00:33:51,180 --> 00:33:52,580
They claim it could go even faster

525
00:33:52,680 --> 00:33:54,580
if it weren't for the short distance of their track.

526
00:33:57,720 --> 00:33:59,960
Germany has poured more than a billion dollars

527
00:34:00,060 --> 00:34:02,020
into the Transrapid program.

528
00:34:02,120 --> 00:34:03,960
But the country recently scrapped plans

529
00:34:03,961 --> 00:34:07,700
to build a maglev between Berlin and Hamburg.

530
00:34:07,800 --> 00:34:11,000
The cost of the planned 185-mile-long guideway

531
00:34:11,100 --> 00:34:14,100
was cited as being too high.

532
00:34:14,200 --> 00:34:17,600
If you try and do something exotic that's different,

533
00:34:17,700 --> 00:34:19,700
that's not compatible with what you've got,

534
00:34:19,800 --> 00:34:22,040
you've got to spend humongous amounts of money

535
00:34:22,140 --> 00:34:23,540
because you have to build it all.

536
00:34:23,640 --> 00:34:26,420
That's one of the issues with maglev.

537
00:34:26,520 --> 00:34:30,120
You have to build the entire system out

538
00:34:30,220 --> 00:34:33,220
to get where you want to go.

539
00:34:33,221 --> 00:34:39,060
In 2001, TransRapid exported its maglev technology to China.

540
00:34:39,061 --> 00:34:42,460
The government plans to build a maglev line that will carry 600

541
00:34:42,461 --> 00:34:46,460
travelers 18 miles from the Shanghai airport to its

542
00:34:46,461 --> 00:34:50,060
financial district. The trip is scheduled to take just

543
00:34:50,061 --> 00:34:54,780
seven minutes on trains that will run over 200 miles an hour.

544
00:34:54,781 --> 00:34:57,980
Shanghai could see its first commercial maglev up and running

545
00:34:57,981 --> 00:35:03,060
by 2003.

546
00:35:03,220 --> 00:35:06,820
After nearly 30 years of research, Japan is still working

547
00:35:06,821 --> 00:35:11,220
to perfect its maglev. Today's experiments are conducted

548
00:35:11,221 --> 00:35:16,820
on this 27-mile track west of Tokyo. The testing that's

549
00:35:16,821 --> 00:35:22,820
scheduled there is expected to take several more years.

550
00:35:22,821 --> 00:35:25,820
In the US, however, a maglev system could be operating much

551
00:35:25,821 --> 00:35:30,820
earlier. A company called American Maglev broke

552
00:35:30,821 --> 00:35:34,160
broke ground on a new maglev system that will carry students

553
00:35:34,260 --> 00:35:36,460
across campus at Old Dominion University

554
00:35:36,560 --> 00:35:37,660
in Norfolk, Virginia.

555
00:35:40,760 --> 00:35:44,060
As many as 100 students will board this vehicle.

556
00:35:44,160 --> 00:35:46,340
It will travel a distance of about a mile

557
00:35:46,440 --> 00:35:49,000
and complete its journey in two minutes and three seconds.

558
00:35:52,180 --> 00:35:54,040
And there could be more maglev projects

559
00:35:54,140 --> 00:35:56,480
here in the United States.

560
00:35:56,580 --> 00:35:58,340
Cities are vying for development money

561
00:35:58,440 --> 00:35:59,920
from the Department of Transportation

562
00:35:59,921 --> 00:36:02,820
to develop their own maglev systems.

563
00:36:02,920 --> 00:36:06,220
We were given the authority and some funds

564
00:36:06,320 --> 00:36:10,960
to begin choosing places where a maglev demonstration project

565
00:36:11,060 --> 00:36:12,360
would actually work.

566
00:36:12,460 --> 00:36:16,740
The two sites that have been chosen for further development

567
00:36:16,840 --> 00:36:21,180
are Baltimore, Washington, and Pittsburgh.

568
00:36:25,180 --> 00:36:26,920
But critics believe that maglevs

569
00:36:26,921 --> 00:36:31,320
offer few advantages over high-speed rail trains.

570
00:36:31,420 --> 00:36:33,620
The main image that Maglev has is

571
00:36:33,720 --> 00:36:37,660
that it is much higher speed than conventional rail

572
00:36:37,760 --> 00:36:39,760
and high-speed rail.

573
00:36:39,860 --> 00:36:43,680
That used to be true maybe 30 years ago when railroads

574
00:36:43,780 --> 00:36:46,580
were traveling up to 100 miles per hour only.

575
00:36:46,680 --> 00:36:49,280
Now, with the advancement of railroads,

576
00:36:49,380 --> 00:36:53,420
the difference between the two is really very small.

577
00:36:53,520 --> 00:36:55,220
The future of Maglev development

578
00:36:55,221 --> 00:36:58,880
remains uncertain, but the quest for better passenger travel

579
00:36:58,881 --> 00:37:03,760
continues, and tomorrow's trains might well travel under oceans

580
00:37:03,761 --> 00:37:05,760
and link our hemispheres.

581
00:37:09,760 --> 00:37:12,580
NASA is designing a maglev launcher.

582
00:37:12,581 --> 00:37:16,180
It would allow space vehicles to reach a speed of 600 miles

583
00:37:16,181 --> 00:37:20,180
per hour and then take off like airplanes.

584
00:37:20,181 --> 00:37:24,620
Bullet Trains will return on Modern Marvels.

585
00:37:25,220 --> 00:37:32,220
We now return to bullet trains on Modern Marvels.

586
00:37:32,221 --> 00:37:36,220
The 21st century has arrived, and futurists are pondering

587
00:37:36,221 --> 00:37:42,220
what form the next generation of high speed trains will take.

588
00:37:42,221 --> 00:37:45,220
In Switzerland, plans are underway to develop what's

589
00:37:45,221 --> 00:37:48,220
called the Swiss Metro Project.

590
00:37:48,221 --> 00:37:51,220
The idea is to propel pressurized levitation vehicles

591
00:37:51,221 --> 00:37:56,220
vehicles at 250 miles an hour in tube tunnels located deep

592
00:37:56,320 --> 00:37:58,220
in the Earth.

593
00:37:58,320 --> 00:38:00,020
All air in the tunnels is removed

594
00:38:00,120 --> 00:38:01,220
to create a partial vacuum.

595
00:38:04,560 --> 00:38:07,980
The vehicle will be propelled by a system similar to a maglev.

596
00:38:11,480 --> 00:38:14,380
Electromagnets will be used to levitate and guide it.

597
00:38:17,680 --> 00:38:19,420
Switching the trains to different tunnels

598
00:38:19,421 --> 00:38:25,420
will be achieved by a mechanism similar to the chamber of a gun.

599
00:38:25,520 --> 00:38:28,420
The creators of Swiss Metro envision a system of tunnels

600
00:38:28,520 --> 00:38:33,160
linking all its major cities and eventually all of Europe.

601
00:38:33,260 --> 00:38:37,340
They also believe it could be used to link continents.

602
00:38:37,440 --> 00:38:40,440
A preliminary study shows that the Swiss Metro is technically

603
00:38:40,540 --> 00:38:43,580
feasible and economically viable, and backers

604
00:38:43,680 --> 00:38:48,780
hope to link at least two cities by 2010.

605
00:38:48,781 --> 00:38:50,340
But those who follow the industry

606
00:38:50,440 --> 00:38:51,980
predict that the train of the future

607
00:38:52,080 --> 00:38:53,320
will be a little less exotic.

608
00:38:57,120 --> 00:39:00,520
Because building a new rail infrastructure is so costly,

609
00:39:00,620 --> 00:39:03,320
today's trend is to get high speed or high performance

610
00:39:03,420 --> 00:39:05,440
trains on non-high speed rail.

611
00:39:09,840 --> 00:39:13,480
France's TGVs are capable of running on both AC and DC

612
00:39:13,580 --> 00:39:17,440
power, as well as three different electrical systems,

613
00:39:17,441 --> 00:39:20,840
so that they can run on other older, non-high-speed lines

614
00:39:20,841 --> 00:39:26,880
that are electrified. Japan has imported high-speed

615
00:39:26,881 --> 00:39:30,480
diesel trains from England to run on its older, non-electrified

616
00:39:30,481 --> 00:39:37,400
routes. In the United States, efforts

617
00:39:37,401 --> 00:39:41,200
are also underway to get faster trains on slower, non-electrified

618
00:39:41,201 --> 00:39:46,400
rail lines. At the Transportation

619
00:39:46,401 --> 00:39:49,640
Center in Pueblo, Colorado, tests were conducted on a

620
00:39:49,641 --> 00:39:53,040
turbine electric train designed by the Canadian manufacturer

621
00:39:53,041 --> 00:39:56,540
Bombardier. The train is powered by a jet

622
00:39:56,541 --> 00:40:00,640
engine turbine and is capable of operating at 150 miles per

623
00:40:00,641 --> 00:40:04,760
hour. That performance puts it in the

624
00:40:04,761 --> 00:40:09,560
company of many of today's high-speed trains.

625
00:40:09,561 --> 00:40:13,160
The turbine engines that are out there are putting out much

626
00:40:13,161 --> 00:40:19,100
out much more power with much less fuel use and pollution.

627
00:40:19,101 --> 00:40:21,860
That has a lot of appeal for urban areas that are looking

628
00:40:21,861 --> 00:40:25,100
at these kind of intercity travel.

629
00:40:25,101 --> 00:40:27,540
The states of Florida, California, Michigan,

630
00:40:27,541 --> 00:40:30,380
and others are all working to develop their own high speed

631
00:40:30,381 --> 00:40:31,081
lines.

632
00:40:33,980 --> 00:40:37,560
Many states are looking at any variety of options.

633
00:40:37,561 --> 00:40:40,660
There are some places that maglev is going to work.

634
00:40:40,661 --> 00:40:44,700
There are other places where an electrified system would make

635
00:40:44,701 --> 00:40:45,760
more sense.

636
00:40:45,761 --> 00:40:49,600
And then there are other places in the country where a non-electric

637
00:40:49,601 --> 00:40:51,100
version would probably make more sense.

638
00:40:55,180 --> 00:40:57,780
European companies hope to build trains for this new

639
00:40:57,781 --> 00:40:59,520
American market.

640
00:40:59,521 --> 00:41:02,740
Bombardier and French company Alstom collaborated on the

641
00:41:02,741 --> 00:41:03,680
Acela.

642
00:41:06,360 --> 00:41:10,080
And now the manufacturers Talgo in Spain.

643
00:41:10,081 --> 00:41:13,680
and Siemens, builders of Germany's trains,

644
00:41:13,681 --> 00:41:16,320
are competing to build other high-speed trains

645
00:41:16,321 --> 00:41:18,880
in this country.

646
00:41:18,881 --> 00:41:20,660
Whether the high-speed train of the future

647
00:41:20,661 --> 00:41:29,500
is electric or turbine, diesel, or propelled by magnets,

648
00:41:29,501 --> 00:41:33,000
it will be designed to wow and woo customers.

649
00:41:33,001 --> 00:41:35,440
In a long-distance train or a high-speed train,

650
00:41:35,441 --> 00:41:36,700
it's very different.

651
00:41:36,701 --> 00:41:39,980
It should be a richer experience with perhaps more textures

652
00:41:39,981 --> 00:41:41,380
or more color.

653
00:41:41,381 --> 00:41:45,020
I go after creating an environment that will really

654
00:41:45,021 --> 00:41:47,520
inspire you, and you're going to want to tell your friends,

655
00:41:47,521 --> 00:41:50,760
you should ride that train.

656
00:41:50,761 --> 00:41:52,700
The French with the TGV have always

657
00:41:52,701 --> 00:41:54,800
known the importance of design and have used it

658
00:41:54,801 --> 00:41:57,300
to market their trains.

659
00:41:57,301 --> 00:42:00,600
When you board a TGV today, you feel like you're not

660
00:42:00,601 --> 00:42:02,660
boarding any train.

661
00:42:02,661 --> 00:42:05,800
You're boarding a very, very different type of train.

662
00:42:05,801 --> 00:42:09,480
So it's hard to explain, but I think it is, in a way,

663
00:42:09,481 --> 00:42:14,520
at the core also of the success of the product.

664
00:42:14,620 --> 00:42:16,220
Japan is no different.

665
00:42:16,320 --> 00:42:19,120
From the first Bullitt train in 1964

666
00:42:19,220 --> 00:42:22,920
to the Nozami 700 series, their trains convey speed

667
00:42:23,020 --> 00:42:24,320
and efficiency.

668
00:42:29,140 --> 00:42:33,940
Alstom's AGV is a new train that hasn't yet hit the market.

669
00:42:34,040 --> 00:42:36,980
It was designed not only for high speed performance,

670
00:42:36,981 --> 00:42:39,380
but also to promote an image of speed and comfort.

671
00:42:42,180 --> 00:42:44,080
Railroads have been with us a long time,

672
00:42:44,180 --> 00:42:47,260
probably be here a long time to come.

673
00:42:47,360 --> 00:42:48,200
Will they look different?

674
00:42:48,300 --> 00:42:49,260
Most likely.

675
00:42:49,360 --> 00:42:50,860
But the basic need is still there.

676
00:42:50,960 --> 00:42:53,300
It's a seat that's environmentally comfortable,

677
00:42:53,400 --> 00:42:56,200
heating, cooling, some degree of food service

678
00:42:56,300 --> 00:42:57,400
if the trip is long enough.

679
00:42:57,500 --> 00:42:58,940
These are basic comforts.

680
00:42:59,040 --> 00:43:01,700
The transportation system that meets those needs

681
00:43:01,800 --> 00:43:04,300
will be a success.

682
00:43:04,400 --> 00:43:06,440
For the high-speed rail industry in Europe

683
00:43:06,441 --> 00:43:10,180
It used to be all about improving travel time.

684
00:43:10,280 --> 00:43:13,080
Today's speed is no longer the focus.

685
00:43:13,180 --> 00:43:16,180
Like the automobile and passenger jet industries,

686
00:43:16,280 --> 00:43:19,680
train companies are focusing on fine-tuning their products,

687
00:43:19,780 --> 00:43:22,520
making them more cost-efficient and safer.

688
00:43:22,620 --> 00:43:26,500
In fact, many believe there is a point when fast is too fast.

689
00:43:26,600 --> 00:43:30,500
It's very exciting to be able to go faster and even faster.

690
00:43:30,600 --> 00:43:34,600
But there will be a point where certain speeds

691
00:43:34,601 --> 00:43:37,600
are unreasonable because the communities that you're

692
00:43:37,700 --> 00:43:41,940
serving are too close for that speed for the ride

693
00:43:42,040 --> 00:43:44,340
to be worth the ride.

694
00:43:44,440 --> 00:43:47,840
There's also a financial consideration.

695
00:43:47,940 --> 00:43:50,180
The cost of maintenance on both train and track

696
00:43:50,280 --> 00:43:52,280
significantly rises with speed.

697
00:43:56,020 --> 00:43:58,820
But as manufacturers and designers work through these

698
00:43:58,920 --> 00:44:01,820
problems, the number of high-speed train networks

699
00:44:01,920 --> 00:44:04,600
will continue to expand.

700
00:44:04,601 --> 00:44:08,600
Europe and Asia show tremendous growth.

701
00:44:08,700 --> 00:44:10,740
What will happen in the United States

702
00:44:10,840 --> 00:44:13,500
is still up for speculation.

703
00:44:13,600 --> 00:44:16,640
We can only assume that like today's bullet trains,

704
00:44:16,740 --> 00:44:19,840
trains of the future in America and the rest of the world

705
00:44:19,940 --> 00:44:23,660
will get us to our destination safely, in comfort,

706
00:44:23,760 --> 00:44:26,060
and faster than we ever dreamed possible.

